    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Isabella won the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> through a civil war, a secret marriage to Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> in 1469 and the War of the Castilian Succession, ended at Alcáçovas in 1479.</li><li>With Ferdinand she restored royal finances and justice, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> in 1492, sponsored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s voyage that year and so began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>.</li><li>Her policy of religious unity brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a>, the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in 1492 and the forced conversion of Muslims after 1502, and her cause for sainthood was suspended in 1991 over the expulsion.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1893 Isabella became the first woman featured on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> postage stamps and the first named woman on a United States coin, the Isabella quarter, both marking 400 years since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s first voyage.</p></aside>
    </section>

