    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1451</span><span class="rf-text">Born at Madrigal de las Altas Torres, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-castile">John II of Castile</a> and Isabella of Portugal</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1468</span><span class="rf-text">At Toros de Guisando, her half-brother Henry IV names her his heir presumptive</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1469</span><span class="rf-text">Marries Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s knowledge</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1474</span><span class="rf-text">Proclaimed Queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and León at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovia">Segovia</a> after Henry IV's death</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1479</span><span class="rf-text">The treaties of Alcáçovas end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-castilian-succession">War of the Castilian Succession</a> and secure her throne</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1492</span><span class="rf-text">Enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> with Ferdinand, issues the Alhambra Decree expelling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and sponsors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s first voyage</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1502</span><span class="rf-text">The Catholic Monarchs break the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and order Muslims to become Christians or leave</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1504</span><span class="rf-text">Withdraws from government and dies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina-del-campo">Medina del Campo</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Isabella was born on 22 April 1451 at Madrigal de las Altas Torres, the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and his second wife, Isabella of Portugal. She stood second in line to the throne behind her childless half-brother Henry, and third after her brother Alfonso was born in 1453. When her father died in 1454 Henry became king as Henry IV, and Isabella, Alfonso and their mother moved to Arévalo, where they lived in poor conditions and short of money, for Henry did not pay what their father's will provided. Under her mother she was taught practical piety and a deep reverence for religion.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s queen, Joan of Portugal, was about to give birth to their daughter Joanna, Isabella and Alfonso were summoned to court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovia">Segovia</a>, and Isabella joined the queen's household. Her education there ranged from grammar, history, rhetoric and philosophy to French, Italian and Portuguese, music, needlework, riding, hunting and falconry, but Henry rarely let her leave Segovia. Her marriage was a question from childhood. At six she was betrothed to Ferdinand, younger son of John II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, but the alliance soon lapsed; a plan to marry her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-prince-of-viana">Charles of Viana</a> ended when his father imprisoned him. In 1465 she refused consent to a match with Afonso V of Portugal, and a betrothal to Pedro Girón, Master of Calatrava, ended when he fell ill and died on his way to meet her.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-castile">John II of Castile<span>Father</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon<span>Married</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1464 a faction of nobles led by Juan Pacheco and Alfonso Carrillo spread rumours that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s daughter Joanna was the child of the royal favourite Beltrán de la Cueva, and civil war broke out in 1465 after they deposed Henry in effigy outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a> and crowned Alfonso. When Alfonso died at fourteen in July 1468, his supporters suspecting poison, the nobles asked Isabella to lead the rebellion. She preferred a settlement: at Toros de Guisando Henry recognised her as heir presumptive on 19 September 1468, and she agreed not to marry without his consent, though he could not force a match on her. When Henry pressed marriages to Afonso V and to Charles, Duke of Berry, she refused and secretly promised herself to Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>. They were married at Valladolid on 19 October 1469 with a supposed papal bull of Pius II, who had died in 1464, obtained with the help of Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Rodrigo Borgia</a>. Henry disinherited her and in 1470 declared Joanna his heir.</p><p>Henry died on 11 December 1474, and on 13 December Isabella was proclaimed queen at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovia">Segovia</a>. Supporters of Joanna married her to Afonso V of Portugal, who invaded in May 1475. At the Battle of Toro on 1 March 1476 both sides claimed victory, but Joanna's party broke up and the Portuguese withdrew. The treaties of Alcáçovas of 4 September 1479 confirmed Isabella's throne in exchange for almost all the disputed Atlantic territories except the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a> and a large war indemnity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Isabella found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in debt and disorder after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s reign. In 1476 she brought the local brotherhoods under the crown as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-hermandad">Santa Hermandad</a>, a police force paid by a tax of 1,800 maravedís on every hundred households, and officials she sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> in 1481 drove out over 1,500 robbers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> of 1480 resumed royal lands Henry had sold below their value, except gifts to churches, hospitals and the poor; she cut the mints, which had grown from five to 150, and fixed a standard for the coinage. The Royal Council was given to lawyers of the lesser nobility and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>, the monarchs heard complaints in person on Fridays, and the jurist Alfonso Díaz de Montalvo compiled the Ordenanzas Reales.</p><p>In 1482 the Catholic Monarchs began the war against the Nasrid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, the last Muslim state of the peninsula. With soldiers from many countries and the best artillery, they took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronda">Ronda</a> in 1485, Málaga, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baza">Baza</a> in 1489, and on 2 January 1492 entered Granada, whose principal mosque was made a church; the Treaty of Granada promised its Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> peace. Three months later Isabella agreed to sponsor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>, who sailed on 3 August 1492 and reached the New World on 12 October. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> of 1494 divided the world beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with Portugal. In 1492 she also endorsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-nebrija">Antonio de Nebrija</a>'s grammar of the Castilian language, which Nebrija meant to teach to the peoples of the new lands.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Isabella and Ferdinand pursued religious and national unity through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a>, with the Dominican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-de-torquemada">Tomás de Torquemada</a> as the first Inquisitor General. Though Isabella opposed harsh measures against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> on economic grounds, Torquemada convinced Ferdinand, and on 31 March 1492 the Alhambra Decree gave the Jews four months to leave, forbidding them to take gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, money, arms or horses. It was long claimed that 200,000 left; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-kamen">Henry Kamen</a> has argued that of about 80,000 Jews at most 40,000 left and the rest converted, and hundreds who stayed were investigated by the Inquisition. A Muslim uprising in 1499 was triggered by the monarchs' policy of forced conversions, and in 1502 they broke the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and ordered Muslims to become Christians or leave.</p><p>She opposed the enslavement of the American natives, holding them subjects of the crown, though prisoners of war and those accused of cannibalism or sodomy could still be enslaved; when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> captured 1,200 men she ordered them returned and his arrest. Contemporaries such as the chronicler Hernando del Pulgar said she followed the path of rigour more than mercy. Antonio Rumeu de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Armas</a> argues that at Alcáçovas the monarchs bought peace at an excessively expensive price. Under the Catholic Monarchs the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> lost political power, becoming an almost passive body. Some authors claim that her reputation for sanctity derives largely from an image she shaped and spread herself.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a>, whose conduct Isabella did not approve, granted her and Ferdinand the title of Catholic Monarchs. Her confessor Cisneros, named Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, reformed the religious institutions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and, as chancellor, gained more and more power. In 1500 she granted all non-rebellious natives in the colonies citizenship and legal freedom by decree. Her court drew learned women, and she made Luisa de Medrano her protégée, who later taught <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-salamanca">University of Salamanca</a>. The monarchs tied Spain to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s dynasties: by 1497 their son John was married to Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, their daughter Isabella to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-of-portugal">Manuel I of Portugal</a> and Joanna to Philip of Austria, and later Maria married Manuel and Catherine of Aragon married Arthur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a>, and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>.</p><p>Her plans for succession failed. Her mother died in 1496, her only son John in 1497 soon after his marriage, and her daughter Isabella in 1498 giving birth to Miguel da Paz, who died at two. Isabella declined after these deaths. She withdrew from government on 14 September 1504 and died on 26 November at the Royal Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina-del-campo">Medina del Campo</a>, of uterine cancer leading to dropsy. In her will she told her heirs not to allow the Indians "to receive any wrong in their persons and property". She was entombed in the Capilla Real in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> beside Ferdinand, and her crowns passed to her daughter Joanna and Philip I.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Isabella and Ferdinand were the first monarchs to be called queen and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and their marriage created the basis for its de facto unification. Their reign completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, which ultimately led to the creation of most of the nations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and it opened the way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-golden-age">Spanish Golden Age</a>. Under her patronage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-fernandez-de-cordoba">Gonzalo Fernández de Córdoba</a> began a military career that changed the organisation and tactics of the Spanish army. Her reign also saw the dawn of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a>. The monarchs' motto, "Tanto Monta", referred to their agreement as equal rulers.</p><p>Her contemporaries praised her: Andrés Bernáldez called her prudent, wise, chaste and devout, and Ferdinand declared in his testament that she was exemplary in every virtue. Her cause for sainthood was opened in 1958 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Valladolid, and a commission reported in 1970 that it had found no act of hers not inspired by Christian criteria; she was named Servant of God in 1974. In 1991 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> suspended the cause because of her expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. The Spanish crown founded the Order of Isabella the Catholic in her honour in 1815, and in 1893 she appeared on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> stamps and coins for the four hundredth anniversary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s voyage. She has been portrayed in films, in the Spanish television series Isabel and in video games.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Isabella I took the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1474 after a civil war and a disputed succession, and with Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> reformed its finances and justice, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and sponsored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>. Her drive for religious unity brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a>, the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and forced conversions of Muslims, and her cause for sainthood was suspended in 1991.</em></p>

